  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA cold front will continue to sweep southeast across the region \ntoday. Showers and thunderstorms over Lake Erie will eventually move \nonshore early this morning and sink south across Northern Ohio and \nNorthwest Pennsylvania through this morning. The front will stall \nsouth of the forecast area this evening with additional rounds of \nshowers and thunderstorms continuing as shortwaves aloft move over \nthe region. There remains some potential for an isolated strong to \nsevere storm along and south of US-30 this afternoon and evening. \nSPC has placed that portion of the forecast area in the SWODY1 \nMarginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) for the threat of damaging wind gusts. \nHard to flush out specifics regarding timing and placement of \nadditional rounds beyond this evening given discrepancies within the \nvarious 00Z CAMs. Maintained at least a chance PoP in the forecast \nwith this update. \n\nAdditionally, heavy rainfall remains a threat as showers and storms \ntrain over the same locations and with area PWATs rising into the \n2.0-2.25 inch range. WPC has included the southern half of the \nforecast area in a Slight Risk (level 2 of 4) for excessive rainfall \ntoday. Both the HREF and REFS LPMM/PMM highlight the potential for a \nlocally higher swath of heavy rainfall along the US 30 corridor. \n\nThe front will sag south during the day Thursday as Canadian high \npressure builds overhead from the north. The forecast is continuing \nto trend drier Thursday night through Friday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nHigh pressure exits to the east Saturday as a low moves east across \nthe Upper Midwest into the Great Lakes. The low will lift a warm \nfront northeast across the region Saturday before another cold front \nsinks southeast Saturday night into Sunday. Main concern for this \ntimeframe will be the heavy rainfall threat. Area PWATs remain high \nwith the potential for 1-3 inches of rainfall across Lower Michigan \ninto Northern Ohio. WPC has maintained a Slight Risk (Level 2 of 4) \nacross the western half of the forecast area for Saturday.",
